{
  "image_name": "2ddc63326ff7ffcf5b915dac5cdc0efd.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes a hierarchical model of the cyanobacterial circadian clock and compares two levels of timing control. In both schematics, the central feature is a cyclic Kai protein nanocomplex in which KaiC interacts sequentially with KaiA and KaiB, forming a post-translational oscillator. This protein cycle is embedded within a broader transcription–translation feedback loop involving the kaiABC gene cluster, mRNAs, and new protein monomers.\n\nThe left side emphasizes that the Kai complex not only feeds back to regulate its own gene expression, but also signals through SasA and Rpa to influence chromosome topology and, more broadly, promoter activity and global gene expression. Arrows indicate that rhythmic changes in the Kai complex propagate outward to many genes.\n\nThe right side proposes an additional layer nested inside the same system: an ATPase cycle within KaiC. This ATPase activity appears as an inner red loop coupled to the larger Kai protein cycle and linked to the same downstream output pathway. Compared with the left panel, the transcriptional components are visually muted, suggesting that ATPase-driven post-translational timing may be more central, while genome-wide transcriptional rhythms are a downstream consequence rather than the primary clock mechanism.",
